1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,337
Aarav opens an old radio.

2
00:00:02,337 --> 00:00:07,077
Inside are tiny beads with coloured
stripes, and not a single number.

3
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:09,585
So how do you know what each one is?

4
00:00:09,585 --> 00:00:11,127
The stripes are the label.

5
00:00:11,627 --> 00:00:13,360
These beads are resistors.

6
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:16,808
A resistor's job is to limit
the current in a circuit.

7
00:00:17,158 --> 00:00:21,268
They are too small for printed numbers,
and printed numbers can rub off

8
00:00:21,268 --> 00:00:22,929
or hide on the far side.

9
00:00:23,279 --> 00:00:27,926
Colour bands go all the way around,
so you can read them from any side.

10
00:00:27,926 --> 00:00:30,114
This is the resistor colour code.

11
00:00:30,764 --> 00:00:33,000
Ten colours stand for the ten digits.

12
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:37,381
Black is zero, brown one, red
two, orange three, yellow four.

13
00:00:37,731 --> 00:00:41,684
Green five, blue six, violet
seven, grey eight, white nine.

14
00:00:42,034 --> 00:00:46,498
To remember the order, say: B, B,
Roy of Great Britain had a Very Good

15
00:00:46,498 --> 00:00:47,398
Wife.

16
00:00:47,014 --> 00:00:49,232
Each capital letter starts a colour.

17
00:00:49,882 --> 00:00:52,218
Two more colours are never digits.

18
00:00:52,218 --> 00:00:53,463
Gold and silver.

19
00:00:53,813 --> 00:00:57,609
They show up only as a
multiplier, or as the tolerance.

20
00:00:58,259 --> 00:01:00,830
Now, how do you read a four band resistor?

21
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:04,820
First, turn it so the gold or
silver band is on the right.

22
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:07,656
It usually sits a little
apart from the others.

23
00:01:08,006 --> 00:01:10,228
The first band is the first digit.

24
00:01:10,228 --> 00:01:12,545
The second band is the second digit.

25
00:01:13,245 --> 00:01:15,450
The third band is the multiplier.

26
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:17,871
It tells you how many zeros to add.

27
00:01:18,571 --> 00:01:20,755
The fourth band is the tolerance.

28
00:01:20,755 --> 00:01:24,948
It tells you how close the real
value can be to the marked value.

29
00:01:25,648 --> 00:01:30,959
So R equals the two digits, times ten
to the power of the multiplier, plus

30
00:01:30,959 --> 00:01:32,755
or minus the tolerance.

31
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:34,496
Resistance is in ohms.

32
00:01:35,146 --> 00:01:37,083
Let's test one on the breadboard.

33
00:01:37,433 --> 00:01:39,288
Yellow, violet, red, gold.

34
00:01:39,638 --> 00:01:42,160
Yellow is four, violet is seven.

35
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:43,786
Red means two zeros.

36
00:01:44,136 --> 00:01:49,154
That is four thousand seven hundred
ohms, or four point seven kilo ohms,

37
00:01:49,154 --> 00:01:51,214
plus or minus five percent.

38
00:01:51,864 --> 00:01:54,187
Connect it to a nine volt battery.

39
00:01:54,187 --> 00:01:57,353
The meter shows about one
point nine milliamperes.

40
00:01:57,703 --> 00:02:00,315
Now swap in brown, black, orange.

41
00:02:00,315 --> 00:02:02,544
One, zero, and three zeros.

42
00:02:02,544 --> 00:02:03,743
Ten kilo ohms.

43
00:02:04,093 --> 00:02:07,344
The current drops to zero
point nine milliamperes.

44
00:02:07,344 --> 00:02:10,817
Watch the scope step down
as the resistance goes up.

45
00:02:11,467 --> 00:02:14,744
So what does plus or minus
five percent really mean?

46
00:02:15,094 --> 00:02:19,559
Five percent of four thousand seven
hundred is two hundred and thirty five

47
00:02:19,559 --> 00:02:20,459
ohms.

48
00:02:20,461 --> 00:02:25,041
So the real value can be anywhere from
four thousand four hundred and sixty

49
00:02:25,041 --> 00:02:28,792
five, to four thousand nine
hundred and thirty five ohms.

50
00:02:29,142 --> 00:02:33,082
Measure a batch, and each one
should land inside that window.

51
00:02:33,082 --> 00:02:37,826
Gold means five percent, silver ten
percent, and no band at all means twenty

52
00:02:37,826 --> 00:02:38,726
percent.

53
00:02:39,165 --> 00:02:41,273
The first two bands are digits.

54
00:02:41,273 --> 00:02:43,430
The third band counts the zeros.

55
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:47,018
Precise circuits use five band resistors.

56
00:02:47,368 --> 00:02:51,554
Here the first three bands are digits,
the fourth is the multiplier, and

57
00:02:51,554 --> 00:02:53,271
the fifth is the tolerance.

58
00:02:53,621 --> 00:02:56,108
Brown, black, black, red, brown.

59
00:02:56,108 --> 00:02:58,702
One, zero, zero, times one hundred.

60
00:02:58,702 --> 00:03:01,511
Ten kilo ohms, plus or minus one percent.

61
00:03:02,161 --> 00:03:05,517
There is one more rating,
and it is not in the colours.

62
00:03:05,517 --> 00:03:06,641
The power rating.

63
00:03:06,991 --> 00:03:09,057
Current through a resistor makes heat.

64
00:03:09,057 --> 00:03:11,008
Too much heat, and it burns out.

65
00:03:11,358 --> 00:03:13,746
Power is voltage times current.

66
00:03:13,746 --> 00:03:15,452
P equals V times I.

67
00:03:16,152 --> 00:03:18,832
By Ohm's law, V equals I times R.

68
00:03:19,532 --> 00:03:21,495
So P equals I squared R.

69
00:03:21,495 --> 00:03:25,290
Or, using the voltage, P
equals V squared over R.

70
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:27,315
Power is in watts.

71
00:03:27,315 --> 00:03:32,015
Common ratings are one eighth, one
quarter, one half, one and two watts.

72
00:03:32,015 --> 00:03:34,627
A bigger body can get rid of more heat.

73
00:03:35,277 --> 00:03:36,405
Worked example.

74
00:03:36,405 --> 00:03:40,606
A brown, black, red resistor
is rated one quarter of a watt.

75
00:03:40,956 --> 00:03:43,742
Brown one, black zero, red two zeros.

76
00:03:43,742 --> 00:03:45,636
That is one thousand ohms.

77
00:03:45,986 --> 00:03:49,176
For the largest current,
I squared R equals P.

78
00:03:49,176 --> 00:03:52,026
So I equals the square root of P over R.

79
00:03:52,726 --> 00:03:57,145
That is the square root of zero
point two five, over one thousand.

80
00:03:57,845 --> 00:04:00,148
About fifteen point eight milliamperes.

81
00:04:00,148 --> 00:04:03,547
Push more current than that,
and the resistor overheats.

82
00:04:04,197 --> 00:04:05,662
Now, the exam trap.

83
00:04:06,012 --> 00:04:09,493
Many students read the
third band as a third digit.

84
00:04:09,493 --> 00:04:12,954
Red, violet, orange becomes
two seven three ohms.

85
00:04:12,954 --> 00:04:13,854
Wrong.

86
00:04:13,928 --> 00:04:17,990
In a four band resistor, the
third band is the multiplier.

87
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:21,720
Red, violet, orange is
twenty seven times ten cubed.

88
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:23,424
Twenty seven kilo ohms.

89
00:04:24,074 --> 00:04:25,554
Where is this asked?

90
00:04:25,554 --> 00:04:30,161
JEE Main and NEET have asked students
to decode colour bands, and to find

91
00:04:30,161 --> 00:04:32,663
a safe current from the power rating.

92
00:04:33,013 --> 00:04:37,596
PUC board papers ask for the colour
table, decoding a resistor, and the

93
00:04:37,596 --> 00:04:40,108
meaning of tolerance and power rating.

94
00:04:40,758 --> 00:04:41,658
Try this.

95
00:04:41,578 --> 00:04:46,570
A resistor has bands green, blue,
red, gold, and is rated half a watt.

96
00:04:46,570 --> 00:04:49,762
What is the largest current
it can safely carry?

97
00:04:55,162 --> 00:04:56,062
B.

98
00:04:55,633 --> 00:04:58,279
About nine point four milliamperes.

99
00:04:58,629 --> 00:05:01,339
Green five, blue six, red two zeros.

100
00:05:01,339 --> 00:05:03,999
That is five thousand six hundred ohms.

101
00:05:04,349 --> 00:05:06,980
I equals the square root of P over R.

102
00:05:07,330 --> 00:05:12,187
The square root of zero point five,
over five thousand six hundred, is

103
00:05:12,187 --> 00:05:14,985
about nine point four five milliamperes.

104
00:05:15,635 --> 00:05:17,157
Screenshot this card.

105
00:05:20,357 --> 00:05:22,452
Next, capacitors and capacitance.

106
00:05:22,452 --> 00:05:26,073
How does a tiny part store
charge, and let it out later?
